## Solution

Let me set up notation and derive the residue formula.

**Setup.** Let $w = z - z_0$. Since $f$ has a pole of order $n$ at $z_0$, its Laurent series is
$$f(z) = \sum_{k=-n}^{\infty} a_k w^k, \qquad a_{-n} \neq 0.$$
Decompose $f$ into three parts:
$$f(z) = \underbrace{a_0}_{\text{constant}} + \underbrace{P(w)}_{\text{principal part}} + \underbrace{Q(w)}_{\text{analytic, vanishing}}, \quad P(w)=\sum_{k=1}^{n} a_{-k} w^{-k}, \quad Q(w)=\sum_{k=1}^{\infty} a_k w^k.$$

Then
$$g(z) = e^{f(z)} = e^{a_0}\, e^{P(w)}\, e^{Q(w)}.$$

**Expand each factor.** Since $P$ and $Q$ are (respectively) a finite sum of negative powers and a convergent power series with zero constant term, the exponential series converge absolutely in a punctured neighborhood of $z_0$:

$$e^{P(w)} = \sum_{j_1,\dots,j_n \ge 0} \prod_{k=1}^{n} \frac{a_{-k}^{\,j_k}}{j_k!}\; w^{-\left(j_1 + 2j_2 + \cdots + n j_n\right)},$$

$$e^{Q(w)} = \sum_{m=0}^{\infty} q_m\, w^m, \qquad q_m = \sum_{\substack{\ell_1,\ell_2,\dots \ge 0 \\ \ell_1 + 2\ell_2 + 3\ell_3 + \cdots = m}} \prod_{k=1}^{\infty} \frac{a_k^{\,\ell_k}}{\ell_k!}.$$

(Here $q_0 = 1$, $q_1 = a_1$, $q_2 = a_2 + \tfrac{1}{2}a_1^2$, etc. — the complete Bell-polynomial-type coefficients of $e^{Q}$.)

**Extract the residue.** The residue of $g$ at $z_0$ is the coefficient of $w^{-1}$ in the product $e^{P(w)}e^{Q(w)}$. A term $w^{-S}$ from $e^{P}$ (with $S = j_1 + 2j_2 + \cdots + n j_n$) combines with a term $w^m$ from $e^{Q}$ to give $w^{-1}$ precisely when $m - S = -1$, i.e. $m = S - 1 \ge 0$, requiring $S \ge 1$.

Therefore

$$\boxed{\operatorname{Res}_{z=z_0} g(z) \;=\; e^{a_0}\!\!\sum_{\substack{j_1,\dots,j_n \ge 0 \\ S := j_1 + 2j_2 + \cdots + n j_n \,\ge\, 1}} \left(\prod_{k=1}^{n} \frac{a_{-k}^{\,j_k}}{j_k!}\right) q_{S-1},}$$

where
$$q_m = \sum_{\substack{\ell_1,\ell_2,\dots \ge 0 \\ \ell_1 + 2\ell_2 + 3\ell_3 + \cdots = m}} \prod_{k=1}^{\infty} \frac{a_k^{\,\ell_k}}{\ell_k!}$$

is the coefficient of $w^m$ in $e^{Q(w)} = \exp\!\bigl(\sum_{k\ge 1} a_k w^k\bigr)$.

**Remarks on the structure.**

- The sum is infinite in general (because $Q$ contributes arbitrarily high powers), but it converges absolutely: $e^{P}$ and $e^{Q}$ both converge absolutely in a punctured disk $0<|w|<R$, so their Cauchy product does too.
- The residue depends on **all** Laurent coefficients $a_{-n},\dots,a_{-1},a_0,a_1,a_2,\dots$ — not just the principal part. The principal-part coefficients $a_{-k}$ appear through the $j_k$, the constant $a_0$ factors out as $e^{a_0}$, and the analytic-part coefficients $a_k$ ($k\ge 1$) enter through the $q_m$.
- $g = e^{f}$ has an **essential singularity** at $z_0$ (since $f$ has a pole, $e^{f}$ blows up along some directions and $\to 0$ along others), so a nonzero residue is expected in general.

**Sanity checks.**

1. *Pure simple pole, $f = a_{-1}/w + a_0$* ($n=1$, $Q\equiv 0$, so $q_0=1$, $q_{m\ge 1}=0$). Only $S=1$ (i.e. $j_1=1$) contributes, giving $\operatorname{Res} = e^{a_0} a_{-1}$. Direct: $e^{a_0}e^{a_{-1}/w} = e^{a_0}(1 + a_{-1}/w + \cdots)$, residue $= e^{a_0}a_{-1}$. ✓

2. *Simple pole with linear analytic term, $f = a_{-1}/w + a_0 + a_1 w$* ($n=1$, $Q = a_1 w$, $q_m = a_1^m/m!$). Formula gives
$$\operatorname{Res} = e^{a_0}\sum_{j_1\ge 1} \frac{a_{-1}^{j_1}}{j_1!}\,\frac{a_1^{j_1-1}}{(j_1-1)!}.$$
Direct expansion $e^{a_0}e^{a_{-1}/w}e^{a_1 w} = e^{a_0}\bigl(\sum_p \tfrac{a_{-1}^p}{p!}w^{-p}\bigr)\bigl(\sum_q \tfrac{a_1^q}{q!}w^q\bigr)$, coeff $w^{-1}$ requires $q=p-1$, $p\ge 1$: $e^{a_0}\sum_{p\ge 1}\tfrac{a_{-1}^p}{p!}\tfrac{a_1^{p-1}}{(p-1)!}$. ✓ (This equals $e^{a_0}\sqrt{a_{-1}a_1}\, I_1(2\sqrt{a_{-1}a_1})$ when $a_{-1}a_1>0$, a modified Bessel function — illustrating the series is generally not elementary.)

The boxed formula is the residue of $g(z)=e^{f(z)}$ at $z_0$, expressed in terms of $n$ and the Laurent coefficients $\{a_k\}_{k=-n}^{\infty}$ of $f$.

